{
  "comment": "Public repositories the 'Real repositories' CI job opens in TabStudio, pinned to commits. .NET repositories name their 'solution' and are built: maxErrors is each one's error baseline, which a build may only lower (the job asks to lower the number here when it does; null records the count without a limit). 'startup' names the project TabStudio must choose as the startup project when the solution opens. A 'serve' entry's 'environment' sets variables for the run, as the app's README asks ($(RandomBase64Key) is a new random key, $(TempDirectory) a new folder). Python, Java, Rust, Go, C/C++, Node.js, PHP, Kotlin and Swift repositories name their 'toolchain' (and 'folder', the app's folder when it is not the repository's root): the project setup installs their packages (for a Prisma app, with its generated client), and Ctrl+F5 runs the startup project, which must be called 'program'; 'dotenv' is the .env the README asks for (node-express-realworld's PostgreSQL and BookStack's MySQL are the job's service containers, and F5 applies their Prisma and Laravel migrations). 'services': 'job' sets the repository's Compose file aside, so the job's service containers serve its databases. With 'web', the program must listen and answer that page in the web preview, showing 'expect'; without it, it must exit with code 0 after printing 'expect'.",
